# aimail configuration — copy to etc/aimail.conf (which is gitignored).
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY PROJECT-SPECIFIC FILE. Everything else in this repo is
# generic. The system this replaces had its mailbox root hardcoded in 14 files;
# porting it meant finding all 14.

# Where mail and state live. MUST be outside the repo: mail is machine-local
# coordination, not source, and committing it would put every message in git.
AIMAIL_ROOT="${AIMAIL_ROOT:-$HOME/.aimail}"

# Poll interval in seconds. The loop exits on the first trigger, so a short
# interval costs nothing while idle — an empty cycle is a `find` and a sleep.
# Tokens are spent only when the poller FIRES.
AIMAIL_POLL_INTERVAL="${AIMAIL_POLL_INTERVAL:-5}"

# Delivery size cap, bytes. Past this the remainder is deferred and stays
# queued; it is never consumed. A burst of six long messages would otherwise
# spend a session's context before it had decided anything.
# ⇒ The cap costs throughput, never correctness.
POLLER_DRAIN_MAXB="${POLLER_DRAIN_MAXB:-60000}"

# If this deployment's actual Stop hook is NOT hooks/stop_guard.sh (e.g. it was
# forked/renamed and wired into settings.json under a different filename),
# point `whoami` at that hook's own session->seat state dir so its
# registrations aren't invisible here too (AR-26). Expects seat_<sid> files.
# AIMAIL_EXTERNAL_SEAT_DIR="/path/to/that/hook/state/dir"

# ─── run_canonical_battery.sh (bin/run_canonical_battery.sh) ──────────────────
# This wrapper only makes sense pointed at one specific downstream project's own
# checkouts and interpreter -- there is no sane generic default, so it refuses
# to run at all until these are set here.
# PLATFORM_ROOT="/path/to/the/target/project/checkout"
# POC_ROOT="/path/to/a/second/sibling/checkout/this/fleet/also/manages"
# CONDA_PY="/path/to/the/target/project/conda/env/bin/python3"

# ─── aimail land's must-prove ref (lib/land.sh) ────────────────────────────────
# If both are set, `aimail land` refuses to move PLATFORM_ROOT's own
# PLATFORM_PRODUCTION_REF without a --gate-summary citation that
# bin/check_battery_summary.sh accepts (tree-matched, BATTERY_EXIT=0) for the
# sha being landed. Unset (either one), that pair is simply not in the
# must-prove set -- AIMAIL_LAND_REQUIRE_GATE can still name other pairs
# explicitly regardless of these two.
# PLATFORM_PRODUCTION_REF="refs/heads/main"

# Every non-optional environment-variable NAME the target project's own
# environment-config module requires (names only, never real values -- the
# actual secrets live in that project's own gitignored .env, read at runtime).
# Entirely project-specific; re-derive by grepping that project's config
# module if its required set changes.
# REQUIRED_ENV_NAMES=(
#     ENVIRONMENT
#     EXAMPLE_API_KEY
#     EXAMPLE_DB_NAME
# )

# ─── claimstate.sh / fleet.sh disk watchdog ───────────────────────────────────
# The real repos this fleet's claims/disk watchdog actually care about, space-
# separated. Empty by default (AIMAIL_HOME, this repo's own checkout, is always
# included regardless).
# AIMAIL_CLAIM_REPOS="/path/to/repo/one /path/to/repo/two"
# AIMAIL_DISK_KNOWN_REPOS="/path/to/repo/one /path/to/repo/two /path/to/a/mirror"

# ─── safe_sync.sh (bin/safe_sync.sh) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Frozen backup-tree prefixes this deployment knows about (a moved/retired
# checkout that must never be treated as a live sync target again). Entirely
# deployment-specific; empty by default.
# AIMAIL_FROZEN_REPO_PREFIXES=(
#     "/mnt/some/old/frozen/checkout/root"
# )

# ─── Budget ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Per-account session caps, as percentages. An account not listed uses DEFAULT.
#
# ⚠ WHY PER-ACCOUNT AND NOT ONE NUMBER: one configured threshold once governed
#   every account, but a shared account must park lower than a private one —
#   overrunning there takes tokens from someone who is not in the conversation.
#   Nobody should discover which account they are on from a lockout.
AIMAIL_CAP_DEFAULT="${AIMAIL_CAP_DEFAULT:-90}"
# AIMAIL_CAP_shared=80

# ─── Landing-guard resolvability check (t908, 2026-09-22) ──────────────────────
# Repos whose main-only landing guard (hooks/main_only_landing_guard.sh, installed as a
# `reference-transaction` hook) `aimail session` / `aimail doctor` / `aimail landing-guard`
# verify is INSTALLED AND RESOLVABLE — a dangling symlink or a core.hooksPath into a
# vanished directory makes git run no hooks, silently. Space-separated. Default when
# unset: $POC_ROOT and $PLATFORM_ROOT above; failing both, the repo containing $PWD.
# AIMAIL_LANDING_GUARD_REPOS="${AIMAIL_LANDING_GUARD_REPOS:-$POC_ROOT $PLATFORM_ROOT}"

# ─── Landing-guard per-repo, per-ref allow-list (second-code-lander policy, 2026-09-22) ──
# hooks/main_only_landing_guard.sh refuses a protected-ref update from any seat not on
# that ref's own allow-list -- seat "main" is always allowed, everywhere, unconditionally;
# this only ever ADDS extra landers on top of that. One "<repo-abs-path>|<ref>|<space-
# separated-extra-seats>" entry per rule. A (repo, ref) pair with no matching entry here
# stays "main only", same as if this array were empty/unset.
# Each protected repo's own git config must ALSO point back at this file (the hook is
# symlinked/copied into that OTHER repo, so it cannot find etc/aimail.conf by walking its
# own path) -- once per repo:
#   git config --add main-landing-guard.aimail-home /path/to/this/AIMail/repo
# AIMAIL_LANDING_GUARD_ALLOW=(
#   "/path/to/your/poc-repo|refs/heads/main|librarian"
#   "/path/to/your/platform-repo|refs/heads/your-landing-branch|audit"
# )

# ─── Supervisor liveness guard (the owner 2026-09-22, top budget item) ────────────────────────────
# `aimail fleet watchdog` (cron, every 5 min) checks the supervisor seat after each ramp and wakes
# or resumes it from the session registry; when that fails it escalates to the vice orchestrator
# and to a human seat if one is set, and writes state/ALERT_supervisor_unreachable, which every
# dashboard prints first. The hard stop itself needs no knob: the poller honours an unpark
# exemption only below AIMAIL_SEAT_CAP_<seat> (assistant: 95).
# AIMAIL_VICE_SUPERVISOR="${AIMAIL_VICE_SUPERVISOR:-main}"
# AIMAIL_PINNED_SEATS="assistant main"   # LOCKED to their account (the owner 2026-09-22 17:28); `seat migrate` refuses without --owner-approved
# AIMAIL_HUMAN_ALERT_SEAT=""            # unset today: the owner's own seat is retired -- set one to reach a human
# AIMAIL_SUPERVISOR_WAKE_RETRIES="${AIMAIL_SUPERVISOR_WAKE_RETRIES:-3}"

# ─── Sterility guard ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# A `|`-separated extended-regex alternation of terms that should never appear in a
# tracked file (case-insensitive) -- your own operator's name, company, customer, or
# project. Checked by hooks/sterility_guard.sh (pre-commit, staged diff only) and by
# tests/run.sh's own sterility arm (whole tree). Unset/empty (the default): the check
# is a deliberate no-op -- a fresh install has no terms of its own yet.
# LICENSE is always exempt: a real copyright notice legitimately names a real person.
# AIMAIL_STERILITY_TERMS="YourCompanyName|YourProjectName|your-customer-name"
# ── idle-backlog sweep: external "open or resolved" classifier (optional) ──────────────
# When set, `aimail fleet` sweeps ask this script for each backlog entry's own disposition:
#   python3 $AIMAIL_TODO_ARCHIVE_TOOL --classify <backlog file>
# and expect one "<id>\t<open|resolved>\t<deciding line>" per entry, where <id> is the first
# T-<digits> in the entry's raw heading line, else "NARR-" + sha1(raw heading line incl. newline)[:10].
# Unset (the default): the sweep's built-in mirror of those rules decides, and says so on stderr.
# Verdicts are cached on the backlog file's mtime+size ($STATE_DIR/backlog_classify.cache).
# AIMAIL_TODO_ARCHIVE_TOOL=/path/to/your/backlog_classifier.py
# AIMAIL_TODO_CLASSIFY_TIMEOUT_SEC=30
# AIMAIL_PINNED_SEATS="assistant main"   # LOCKED to their account (the owner 2026-09-22 17:28); `seat migrate` refuses without --owner-approved

# ─── Account pool (_configured_account_pool, lib/budget.sh) ─────────────────────────────────
# The explicit, space-separated set of accounts this fleet actually manages, by name
# (`work research r2`, NOT a path) -- read by `budget pool`, `placement`, and `fleet`'s
# account-dir enumeration, all through one function so they can never disagree.
# ⛔ SET ONE OF THESE. Leaving both unset falls back to "whichever accounts currently have a
#   live, resolved seat" -- a genuinely idle, seatless-but-real account (e.g. parked at its own
#   block boundary with nobody on it right now) is invisible under that fallback until a seat
#   lands there again. Never globs $HOME/.claude-* to guess the pool: on a real machine
#   $HOME/.claude can be a symlink ALIAS to one of the $HOME/.claude-<name> dirs (the same
#   account under two names, which reads as a TWIN and breaks `seat migrate`), and a glob has no
#   way to tell a genuine fleet account from any other, unrelated Claude account dir that
#   happens to exist on the same machine (found live, 2026-09-25: both defects at once).
# AIMAIL_ACCOUNT_POOL="work research r2"    # preferred name for this purpose; wins if both are set
# AIMAIL_FLEET_ACCOUNTS="work research r2"  # same shape, older name, still honoured

# ─── Placement rules (T-917, lib/placement.sh) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# `aimail placement [seat]`, `seat migrate` (refuses a rule-breaking move), the balancer's target,
# and the budget/fleet dashboards all read these. Defaults are right for this fleet.
# AIMAIL_PRECIOUS_ACCOUNT=""      # the supervisor's account; default: the supervisor's seat record (work)
# AIMAIL_FABLE_SEAT="fable"       # placed by its OWN model's weekly headroom (the probe's fable-model weekly)
# AIMAIL_PLACEMENT_SEATS=""       # TEST SEAM only: "acct:seat,seat acct2:seat" overrides the live seat->account map
# AIMAIL_HUMAN_ALERT_SEAT=""            # unset today: the owner's own seat is retired -- set one to reach a human
# AIMAIL_SUPERVISOR_WAKE_RETRIES="${AIMAIL_SUPERVISOR_WAKE_RETRIES:-3}"

# ── T-917 item 3: crossing warnings (lib/warnings.sh) ─────────────────────────
# AIMAIL_WARN_LEVELS="50 80"   the % levels that mail once per (account, gauge, level, window)
# AIMAIL_WARNINGS=1            0 disables the warnings tick inside `budget autopilot`

# ── T-917 item 4: the balancer acts (lib/act.sh) ─────────────────────────────
# AIMAIL_BALANCE_ACT=0             1 = announce-then-do: one seat per autopilot tick, never mid-turn
# AIMAIL_BALANCE_ACT_LEVEL=80      block % that triggers a move (an over-fair-share account also does)
# AIMAIL_BALANCE_ACT_DELAY_MIN=10  minutes between the announcement mail and the move

# ── the ask ledger (lib/ask.sh) — a task the owner asked for is never silently dropped ────────
# AIMAIL_ASK_STALE=1800            seconds untouched before an open row is STALE (one mail per
#                                   episode to its owner + the supervisor; a touch ends the episode)
# AIMAIL_ASK_CHECK_TIMEOUT=30      seconds before a row's --check predicate is killed (not done)
# AIMAIL_ASK_LOCK_WAIT=10          seconds to wait for the ledger's own flock before refusing
# AIMAIL_OWNER_INBOX=""            unset = skipped; a file path gets one STALLED line per row at
#                                   2x the stale window (a human-facing escalation, once per episode)

# ── `aimail land` (lib/land.sh) — the one way to move a shared ref ───────────────────────────
# AIMAIL_LAND_LOCK_WAIT=30         seconds to wait for the per-(repo,ref) land lock before refusing
# AIMAIL_LANDING_GUARD_REQUIRE_FF=1   the reference-transaction hook refuses any non-fast-forward
#                                      (and any deletion) on a protected ref, for every writer
